nia remains the closest thing to a pure tossup on the presidential map. The state's partisan lean is essentially even, tilting Republican by about a point, and the incumbent president is term-limited, leaving both tickets open. No general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so the picture rests on the state's basic partisan balance.\n\nState politics offer the main signal: Governor Josh Shapiro leads his reelection bid by sixteen points, and most Pennsylvania Democrats say they would back him for president, though voters are split on whether he should run. A popular Democratic governor could trim the Republican edge, but gubernatorial strength does not automatically carry into a presidential contest, and the nominees remain unset. Expect a margin near even, with wide uncertainty until polling and the field clarify.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4565, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.5269, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.504, "margin": 0.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.475, "margin": -0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4803, "margin": -0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.5405, "margin": 1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.485, "margin": -0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.4847, "margin": -0.5}]}